{"unit":"13","title":"Patterns","pages":"p.215 to 230","sections":[{"id":"13-1","title":"Problem Solving Involving Patterns","short":"1. Patterns","pages":"p.215 to 224","learn":[{"tag":"Idea","title":"What is a pattern?","html":"<p>A <b>pattern</b> follows the same rule again and again.</p><p>In a <b>number pattern</b>, each number is called a <b>term</b>. Term 1 is the first number, term 2 is the second, and so on.</p><p>Many patterns <b>add the same number</b> each time. We call it the jump.</p>","fig":null,"page":216},{"tag":"Example","title":"How many circles?","html":"<p>Ravi's class saw this pattern on the board. How many circles are there in Figure 65?</p><p>Figure 1: 1 = 1 × 1<br>Figure 2: 4 = 2 × 2<br>Figure 3: 9 = 3 × 3</p><p>These are <b>square numbers</b>. Figure 65 has 65 × 65 = <b>4,225 circles</b>.</p>","fig":{"w":320,"h":110,"pts":{},"items":[{"t":"circle","c":[62.0,70],"r":7,"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"txt","at":[62.0,100],"s":"Figure 1","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"},{"t":"circle","c":[152.0,54],"r":7,"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"circle","c":[136.0,70],"r":7,"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"circle","c":[152.0,70],"r":7,"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"circle","c":[168.0,70],"r":7,"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"txt","at":[152.0,100],"s":"Figure 2","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"},{"t":"circle","c":[250.0,38],"r":7,"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"circle","c":[234.0,54],"r":7,"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"circle","c":[250.0,54],"r":7,"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"circle","c":[266.0,54],"r":7,"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"circle","c":[218.0,70],"r":7,"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"circle","c":[234.0,70],"r":7,"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"circle","c":[250.0,70],"r":7,"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"circle","c":[266.0,70],"r":7,"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"circle","c":[282.0,70],"r":7,"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"txt","at":[250.0,100],"s":"Figure 3","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"}]},"page":215},{"tag":"Idea","title":"Finding any term","html":"<p>Count the jumps! To get to term 5, you make 4 jumps.</p><p>The number of jumps is always <b>1 less</b> than the term number.</p><p>Term = first number + (number of jumps × the jump)</p>","fig":null,"page":216},{"tag":"Example","title":"4, 10, 16, 22, 28, … term 38","html":"<p>The jump is 6.</p><p>Term 1 = 4<br>Term 2 = 4 + (1 × 6) = 10<br>Term 3 = 4 + (2 × 6) = 16<br>Term 5 = 4 + (4 × 6) = 28</p><p>Term 38 = 4 + (37 × 6) = 4 + 222 = <b>226</b></p>","fig":null,"page":216},{"tag":"Idea","title":"Adding in pairs","html":"<p>To add a long list of numbers, pair the <b>first and last</b>, then the next two, and so on.</p><p>Easy start: 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = (1 + 4) + (2 + 3) = 5 + 5 = 10</p>","fig":null,"page":217},{"tag":"Example","title":"All even numbers smaller than 100","html":"<p>2 + 4 + 6 + … + 96 + 98. 100 is not included.</p><p>From 1 to 100, 100 ÷ 2 = 50 numbers are even. Take away 100: 50 - 1 = <b>49 even numbers</b>.</p><p>2 + 96 = 98, 4 + 94 = 98, … 46 + 52 = 98, 48 + 50 = 98. That uses 48 numbers, so 48 ÷ 2 = <b>24 pairs</b> of 98.</p><p>24 × 98 = 2,352. The number 98 is left over: 2,352 + 98 = <b>2,450</b></p>","fig":null,"page":217},{"tag":"Idea","title":"Shape patterns","html":"<p>Look at what is <b>added each time</b>. Make a table: Figure, how many are added, total.</p><p>Then use the same rule as for numbers.</p>","fig":null,"page":218},{"tag":"Example","title":"Peter's toothpick squares","html":"<p>Peter started with 1 toothpick and added more to make squares.</p><p>Figure 1: 1<br>Figure 2: 1 + 3 = 4<br>Figure 3: 1 + 3 + 3 = 7<br>Figure 4: 1 + 3 + 3 + 3 = 10</p><p>Each new square needs <b>3 more</b> toothpicks.</p>","fig":{"w":388,"h":104,"pts":{},"items":[{"t":"seg","p":[[46.0,70],[46.0,40]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"txt","at":[46.0,94],"s":"Figure 1","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"},{"t":"seg","p":[[121.0,40],[151.0,40]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[121.0,70],[121.0,40]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[121.0,70],[151.0,70]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[151.0,70],[151.0,40]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"txt","at":[136.0,94],"s":"Figure 2","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"},{"t":"seg","p":[[196.0,40],[226.0,40]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[196.0,70],[196.0,40]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[196.0,70],[226.0,70]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[226.0,40],[256.0,40]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[226.0,70],[226.0,40]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[226.0,70],[256.0,70]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[256.0,70],[256.0,40]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"txt","at":[226.0,94],"s":"Figure 3","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"},{"t":"seg","p":[[284.0,40],[314.0,40]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[284.0,70],[284.0,40]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[284.0,70],[314.0,70]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[314.0,40],[344.0,40]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[314.0,70],[314.0,40]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[314.0,70],[344.0,70]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[344.0,40],[374.0,40]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[344.0,70],[344.0,40]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[344.0,70],[374.0,70]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[374.0,70],[374.0,40]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"txt","at":[329.0,94],"s":"Figure 4","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"}]},"page":218},{"tag":"Example","title":"Peter: Figures 5 and 10","html":"<p>Figure 5 = 1 + (4 × 3) = 1 + 12 = <b>13 toothpicks</b></p><p>The groups of 3 are 1 less than the Figure number.</p><p>Figure 10 = 1 + (9 × 3) = 1 + 27 = <b>28 toothpicks</b></p>","fig":{"w":320,"h":104,"pts":{},"items":[{"t":"seg","p":[[100.0,40],[130.0,40]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[100.0,70],[100.0,40]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[100.0,70],[130.0,70]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[130.0,40],[160.0,40]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[130.0,70],[130.0,40]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[130.0,70],[160.0,70]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[160.0,40],[190.0,40]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[160.0,70],[160.0,40]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[160.0,70],[190.0,70]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[190.0,40],[220.0,40]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[190.0,70],[190.0,40]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[190.0,70],[220.0,70]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[220.0,70],[220.0,40]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"txt","at":[160.0,94],"s":"Figure 5","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"}]},"page":219},{"tag":"Idea","title":"Hands-On: tiles in 2 colours","html":"<p>Work in pairs. Figure 1 has 1 tile, Figure 2 has 3, Figure 3 has 5.</p><p>Make Figures 4 and 5 with your tiles. Each time, <b>2 tiles</b> are added.</p><p>So Figure 10 = 1 + (9 × 2) = 1 + 18 = <b>19 tiles</b>.</p>","fig":{"w":320,"h":94,"pts":{},"items":[{"t":"poly","p":[[35.0,60],[57.0,60],[57.0,38],[35.0,38]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"txt","at":[46.0,84],"s":"Figure 1","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"},{"t":"poly","p":[[104.0,60],[126.0,60],[126.0,38],[104.0,38]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[126.0,60],[148.0,60],[148.0,38],[126.0,38]],"fill":"#A5D6A7"},{"t":"poly","p":[[148.0,60],[170.0,60],[170.0,38],[148.0,38]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"txt","at":[137.0,84],"s":"Figure 2","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"},{"t":"poly","p":[[196.0,60],[218.0,60],[218.0,38],[196.0,38]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[218.0,60],[240.0,60],[240.0,38],[218.0,38]],"fill":"#A5D6A7"},{"t":"poly","p":[[240.0,60],[262.0,60],[262.0,38],[240.0,38]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[262.0,60],[284.0,60],[284.0,38],[262.0,38]],"fill":"#A5D6A7"},{"t":"poly","p":[[284.0,60],[306.0,60],[306.0,38],[284.0,38]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"txt","at":[251.0,84],"s":"Figure 3","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"}]},"page":220}],"questions":[{"label":"Do and Learn 1","page":220,"q":"Henry uses some toothpicks to make the following pattern: How many toothpicks are there in Figure 10?","fig":{"w":361,"h":94,"pts":{},"items":[{"t":"seg","p":[[31.0,60],[46.0,34.0]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[31.0,60],[61.0,60]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[46.0,34.0],[61.0,60]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"txt","at":[46.0,84],"s":"Figure 1","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"},{"t":"seg","p":[[109.5,60],[124.5,34.0]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[109.5,60],[139.5,60]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[124.5,34.0],[139.5,60]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[124.5,34.0],[154.5,34.0]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[139.5,60],[154.5,34.0]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"txt","at":[132.0,84],"s":"Figure 2","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"},{"t":"seg","p":[[188.0,60],[203.0,34.0]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[188.0,60],[218.0,60]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[203.0,34.0],[218.0,60]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[203.0,34.0],[233.0,34.0]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[218.0,60],[233.0,34.0]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[218.0,60],[248.0,60]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[233.0,34.0],[248.0,60]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"txt","at":[218.0,84],"s":"Figure 3","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"},{"t":"seg","p":[[272.0,60],[287.0,34.0]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[272.0,60],[302.0,60]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[287.0,34.0],[302.0,60]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[287.0,34.0],[317.0,34.0]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[302.0,60],[317.0,34.0]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[302.0,60],[332.0,60]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[317.0,34.0],[332.0,60]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[317.0,34.0],[347.0,34.0]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[332.0,60],[347.0,34.0]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"txt","at":[309.5,84],"s":"Figure 4","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"}]},"hint":"Count the toothpicks in each Figure. How many are added each time?","steps":["Figure 1 = <b>3</b> toothpicks, Figure 2 = <b>5</b>, Figure 3 = <b>7</b>, Figure 4 = <b>9</b>.","Each new triangle adds <b>2 toothpicks</b>.","Figure 10 has 10 - 1 = <b>9 groups</b> of 2 added.","Figure 10 = 3 + (9 × 2) = 3 + 18 = <b>21</b>"],"answer":"21 toothpicks","why":"Start with 3, then add 2 nine times: 3 + 18 = 21."},{"label":"Do and Learn 2 (a)","page":220,"q":"Fill in the missing number. 5, 11, 17, 23, 29, ... From the above pattern, what number is term 50?","fig":null,"hint":"Find the jump. Term 50 has 49 jumps.","steps":["Find the jump: 11 - 5 = <b>6</b>. The pattern adds 6 each time.","Look at the terms:<br>Term 1 is 5 = 5<br>Term 2 is 11 = 5 + (1 × 6)<br>Term 3 is 17 = 5 + (2 × 6)<br>Term 4 is 23 = 5 + (3 × 6)<br>Term 5 is 29 = 5 + (4 × 6)","The number of jumps is always 1 less than the term number. Term 50 has 50 - 1 = <b>49 jumps</b>.","Term 50 = 5 + (49 × 6) = 5 + 294 = <b>299</b>"],"answer":"299","why":"5 + (49 × 6) = 299."},{"label":"Do and Learn 2 (b)","page":220,"q":"Fill in the missing number. 18, 23, 28, 33, 38, ... From the above pattern, what number is term 99?","fig":null,"hint":"Find the jump. Term 99 has 98 jumps.","steps":["Find the jump: 23 - 18 = <b>5</b>. The pattern adds 5 each time.","Look at the terms:<br>Term 1 is 18 = 18<br>Term 2 is 23 = 18 + (1 × 5)<br>Term 3 is 28 = 18 + (2 × 5)<br>Term 4 is 33 = 18 + (3 × 5)<br>Term 5 is 38 = 18 + (4 × 5)","The number of jumps is always 1 less than the term number. Term 99 has 99 - 1 = <b>98 jumps</b>.","Term 99 = 18 + (98 × 5) = 18 + 490 = <b>508</b>"],"answer":"508","why":"18 + (98 × 5) = 508."},{"label":"Exercise 1","page":221,"q":"Look at the following number pattern: 8, 15, 22, 29, 36, ..., (20th term) Write the rule for the pattern and find the missing number.","fig":null,"hint":"What is added each time? The 20th term has 19 jumps.","steps":["Find the jump: 15 - 8 = <b>7</b>. The pattern adds 7 each time.","The rule is to <b>add 7 each time</b>.","Look at the terms:<br>Term 1 is 8 = 8<br>Term 2 is 15 = 8 + (1 × 7)<br>Term 3 is 22 = 8 + (2 × 7)<br>Term 4 is 29 = 8 + (3 × 7)<br>Term 5 is 36 = 8 + (4 × 7)","The number of jumps is always 1 less than the term number. Term 20 has 20 - 1 = <b>19 jumps</b>.","Term 20 = 8 + (19 × 7) = 8 + 133 = <b>141</b>"],"answer":"The rule is to add 7 each time. The missing number is 141.","why":"8 + (19 × 7) = 141."},{"label":"Exercise 2","page":221,"q":"Look at the following number pattern: 13, 21, 29, 37, 45, ..., (25th term) Write the rule for the pattern and find the missing number.","fig":null,"hint":"What is added each time? The 25th term has 24 jumps.","steps":["Find the jump: 21 - 13 = <b>8</b>. The pattern adds 8 each time.","The rule is to <b>add 8 each time</b>.","Look at the terms:<br>Term 1 is 13 = 13<br>Term 2 is 21 = 13 + (1 × 8)<br>Term 3 is 29 = 13 + (2 × 8)<br>Term 4 is 37 = 13 + (3 × 8)<br>Term 5 is 45 = 13 + (4 × 8)","The number of jumps is always 1 less than the term number. Term 25 has 25 - 1 = <b>24 jumps</b>.","Term 25 = 13 + (24 × 8) = 13 + 192 = <b>205</b>"],"answer":"The rule is to add 8 each time. The missing number is 205.","why":"13 + (24 × 8) = 205."},{"label":"Exercise 3","page":222,"q":"Find the sum of all odd numbers that are smaller than 100.","fig":null,"hint":"Pair the first and the last: 1 + 99. Then 3 + 97 ...","steps":["From 1 to 100 there are 100 whole numbers. Half are odd: 100 ÷ 2 = <b>50 odd numbers</b>.","Pair them up: 1 + 99 = 100, 3 + 97 = 100, 47 + 53 = 100, 49 + 51 = 100.","All 50 odd numbers are used, so there are 50 ÷ 2 = <b>25 pairs</b>. Each pair makes 100.","25 × 100 = <b>2,500</b>"],"answer":"2,500","why":"25 pairs, and each pair adds up to 100."},{"label":"Exercise 4","page":222,"q":"A square table has 4 seats at first. Each time a table is joined together, some more seats are added. How many seats are there if there are 30 tables?","fig":{"w":338,"h":122,"pts":{},"items":[{"t":"poly","p":[[45.0,70],[71.0,70],[71.0,44],[45.0,44]],"fill":"#ffffff"},{"t":"circle","c":[58.0,32],"r":6,"fill":"#C8E6C9"},{"t":"circle","c":[58.0,82],"r":6,"fill":"#C8E6C9"},{"t":"circle","c":[33.0,57.0],"r":6,"fill":"#C8E6C9"},{"t":"circle","c":[83.0,57.0],"r":6,"fill":"#C8E6C9"},{"t":"txt","at":[58.0,112],"s":"1 table","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"},{"t":"poly","p":[[136.0,70],[162.0,70],[162.0,44],[136.0,44]],"fill":"#ffffff"},{"t":"poly","p":[[162.0,70],[188.0,70],[188.0,44],[162.0,44]],"fill":"#ffffff"},{"t":"circle","c":[149.0,32],"r":6,"fill":"#C8E6C9"},{"t":"circle","c":[149.0,82],"r":6,"fill":"#C8E6C9"},{"t":"circle","c":[175.0,32],"r":6,"fill":"#C8E6C9"},{"t":"circle","c":[175.0,82],"r":6,"fill":"#C8E6C9"},{"t":"circle","c":[124.0,57.0],"r":6,"fill":"#C8E6C9"},{"t":"circle","c":[200.0,57.0],"r":6,"fill":"#C8E6C9"},{"t":"txt","at":[162.0,112],"s":"2 tables","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"},{"t":"poly","p":[[234.0,70],[260.0,70],[260.0,44],[234.0,44]],"fill":"#ffffff"},{"t":"poly","p":[[260.0,70],[286.0,70],[286.0,44],[260.0,44]],"fill":"#ffffff"},{"t":"poly","p":[[286.0,70],[312.0,70],[312.0,44],[286.0,44]],"fill":"#ffffff"},{"t":"circle","c":[247.0,32],"r":6,"fill":"#C8E6C9"},{"t":"circle","c":[247.0,82],"r":6,"fill":"#C8E6C9"},{"t":"circle","c":[273.0,32],"r":6,"fill":"#C8E6C9"},{"t":"circle","c":[273.0,82],"r":6,"fill":"#C8E6C9"},{"t":"circle","c":[299.0,32],"r":6,"fill":"#C8E6C9"},{"t":"circle","c":[299.0,82],"r":6,"fill":"#C8E6C9"},{"t":"circle","c":[222.0,57.0],"r":6,"fill":"#C8E6C9"},{"t":"circle","c":[324.0,57.0],"r":6,"fill":"#C8E6C9"},{"t":"txt","at":[273.0,112],"s":"3 tables","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"}]},"hint":"Count the seats for 1, 2 and 3 tables. What is the same each time?","steps":["1 table: <b>4</b> seats. 2 tables: <b>6</b> seats. 3 tables: <b>8</b> seats.","Each table has 1 seat on top and 1 below: 2 seats for each table. There is also 1 seat at each end: 2 more.","The rule is 2 + (number of tables) × 2. Check: 2 + (3 × 2) = 8 ✓","30 tables: 2 + (30 × 2) = 2 + 60 = <b>62</b>"],"answer":"62 seats","why":"2 seats for each of the 30 tables, plus 2 at the ends."},{"label":"Exercise 5","page":223,"q":"Ravi made the following pattern using toothpicks. He wrote the number of toothpicks needed in the table below. How many toothpicks are needed to make the 10th figure?<br>Figure 1: 3, Figure 2: 9, Figure 3: 18, Figure 4: 30","fig":{"w":320,"h":134,"pts":{},"items":[{"t":"seg","p":[[42.0,100.0],[56.0,75.8]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[42.0,100.0],[70.0,100.0]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[56.0,75.8],[70.0,100.0]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"txt","at":[56.0,124],"s":"Figure 1","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"},{"t":"seg","p":[[122.0,100.0],[136.0,75.8]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[122.0,100.0],[150.0,100.0]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[136.0,75.8],[150.0,51.5]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[136.0,75.8],[150.0,100.0]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[136.0,75.8],[164.0,75.8]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[150.0,51.5],[164.0,75.8]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[150.0,100.0],[164.0,75.8]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[150.0,100.0],[178.0,100.0]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[164.0,75.8],[178.0,100.0]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"txt","at":[150.0,124],"s":"Figure 2","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"},{"t":"seg","p":[[212.0,100.0],[226.0,75.8]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[212.0,100.0],[240.0,100.0]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[226.0,75.8],[240.0,51.5]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[226.0,75.8],[240.0,100.0]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[226.0,75.8],[254.0,75.8]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[240.0,51.5],[254.0,27.3]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[240.0,51.5],[254.0,75.8]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[240.0,51.5],[268.0,51.5]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[240.0,100.0],[254.0,75.8]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[240.0,100.0],[268.0,100.0]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[254.0,27.3],[268.0,51.5]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[254.0,75.8],[268.0,51.5]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[254.0,75.8],[268.0,100.0]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[254.0,75.8],[282.0,75.8]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[268.0,51.5],[282.0,75.8]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[268.0,100.0],[282.0,75.8]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[268.0,100.0],[296.0,100.0]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"seg","p":[[282.0,75.8],[296.0,100.0]],"color":"#43A047","width":5},{"t":"txt","at":[254.0,124],"s":"Figure 3","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"}]},"hint":"Find how much is added each time: 3 → 9 → 18 → 30. Is the jump the same?","steps":["The jumps are 9 - 3 = <b>6</b>, 18 - 9 = <b>9</b>, 30 - 18 = <b>12</b>. The jump grows by 3 each time: 2 × 3, 3 × 3, 4 × 3.","Figure 5 = 30 + (5 × 3) = <b>45</b><br>Figure 6 = 45 + (6 × 3) = <b>63</b><br>Figure 7 = 63 + (7 × 3) = <b>84</b>","Figure 8 = 84 + (8 × 3) = <b>108</b><br>Figure 9 = 108 + (9 × 3) = <b>135</b><br>Figure 10 = 135 + (10 × 3) = <b>165</b>"],"answer":"165 toothpicks","why":"Each Figure adds a bigger multiple of 3: + 6, + 9, + 12, … + 30."},{"label":"Exercise 6","page":224,"q":"Siti has a box of paper clips. There are 20 paper clips in a box. Each day, she buys 2 more boxes of paper clips than the previous day. On which day would she have 1,280 paper clips?","fig":{"w":320,"h":84,"pts":{},"items":[{"t":"poly","p":[[42.0,50],[62.0,50],[62.0,30],[42.0,30]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"txt","at":[52.0,74],"s":"Day 1","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"},{"t":"poly","p":[[112.0,50],[132.0,50],[132.0,30],[112.0,30]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[132.0,50],[152.0,50],[152.0,30],[132.0,30]],"fill":"#A5D6A7"},{"t":"poly","p":[[152.0,50],[172.0,50],[172.0,30],[152.0,30]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"txt","at":[142.0,74],"s":"Day 2","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"},{"t":"poly","p":[[200.0,50],[220.0,50],[220.0,30],[200.0,30]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[220.0,50],[240.0,50],[240.0,30],[220.0,30]],"fill":"#A5D6A7"},{"t":"poly","p":[[240.0,50],[260.0,50],[260.0,30],[240.0,30]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[260.0,50],[280.0,50],[280.0,30],[260.0,30]],"fill":"#A5D6A7"},{"t":"poly","p":[[280.0,50],[300.0,50],[300.0,30],[280.0,30]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"txt","at":[250.0,74],"s":"Day 3","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"}]},"hint":"Make a table: boxes each day and total boxes. Do the totals look familiar?","steps":["Boxes each day: 1, 3, 5, 7, … (2 more each day).","Total boxes: Day 1 = <b>1</b>, Day 2 = 1 + 3 = <b>4</b>, Day 3 = 4 + 5 = <b>9</b>, Day 4 = 9 + 7 = <b>16</b>. These are square numbers: 1 × 1, 2 × 2, 3 × 3, 4 × 4.","1,280 paper clips ÷ 20 in a box = <b>64 boxes</b>.","8 × 8 = 64, so it is <b>Day 8</b>."],"answer":"Day 8","why":"On Day 8 she has 8 × 8 = 64 boxes, and 64 × 20 = 1,280 paper clips."}],"flash":[{"front":"Term","back":"One number in a number pattern (term 1, term 2, …)"},{"front":"Rule","back":"What the pattern does each time, like 'add 6 each time'"},{"front":"Jumps to reach term 38","back":"37 (always 1 less than the term number)"},{"front":"Term = ?","back":"First number + (number of jumps × the jump)"},{"front":"Square numbers","back":"1, 4, 9, 16, 25, … (1 × 1, 2 × 2, 3 × 3, …)"},{"front":"Sum of odd numbers smaller than 100","back":"2,500 (25 pairs of 100)"},{"front":"Sum of even numbers smaller than 100","back":"2,450 (24 pairs of 98, plus 98)"},{"front":"Peter's toothpick squares","back":"1, 4, 7, 10, … add 3 each time"},{"front":"Henry's toothpick triangles","back":"3, 5, 7, 9, … add 2 each time"},{"front":"Seats around joined tables","back":"2 + (number of tables) × 2"}],"match":[["4, 10, 16, … term 38","226"],["5, 11, 17, … term 50","299"],["18, 23, 28, … term 99","508"],["8, 15, 22, … term 20","141"],["13, 21, 29, … term 25","205"],["Seats for 30 tables","62"],["Peter's Figure 10","28 toothpicks"],["Circles in Figure 65","4,225"]],"quiz":[{"q":"3, 7, 11, 15, … What is term 10?","options":["39","43","40","35"]},{"q":"2, 7, 12, 17, … What is term 20?","options":["97","102","100","95"]},{"q":"What is the rule for 9, 16, 23, 30, …?","options":["Add 7 each time","Add 9 each time","Multiply by 2","Add 6 each time"]},{"q":"How many jumps from term 1 to term 30?","options":["29","30","31","15"]},{"q":"7, 10, 13, 16, … What is term 30?","options":["94","97","90","100"]},{"q":"Peter's toothpicks: 1, 4, 7, 10, … Figure 20?","options":["58","61","60","57"]},{"q":"Which are square numbers?","options":["1, 4, 9, 16","1, 3, 5, 7","2, 4, 6, 8","1, 2, 4, 8"]},{"q":"Figure 15 of the circle pattern (1, 4, 9, …) has how many circles?","options":["225","30","45","29"]},{"q":"Seats around 10 joined tables (rule 2 + tables × 2)?","options":["22","20","40","24"]},{"q":"3, 5, 7, 9, … What is term 25?","options":["51","53","50","75"]}]},{"id":"13-ch","title":"Unit 13 Challenge","short":"⭐ Challenge","pages":"p.225 to 230","learn":[{"tag":"Idea","title":"Unit 13 big ideas","html":"<p>Find the <b>jump</b>. Count the jumps: always <b>1 less</b> than the term or Figure number.</p><p>Term = first number + (jumps × the jump).</p><p>Square numbers: 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, …</p>","fig":null,"page":225},{"tag":"Idea","title":"Performance Task","html":"<p>Work in pairs. Make your own pattern with square tiles that adds the <b>same number of tiles</b> each time.</p><p>Draw Figures 1 to 4, fill in a table (Figure, tiles added, total), then use your rule to find Figure 10.</p>","fig":null,"page":226},{"tag":"Example","title":"Draw a model to check","html":"<p>John: 100 baht, then groups of 40 baht, up to 420 baht.</p><p>420 - 100 = 320<br>320 ÷ 40 = <b>8 groups</b> of 40 baht</p>","fig":null,"page":228},{"tag":"Example","title":"Math in Real Life","html":"<p>Dean has 50 baht at first. He then saves 30 baht from the next day onward. How much will he have after 10 days?</p><p>Day 1: 50<br>Day 2: 50 + 30<br>Day 3: 50 + 30 + 30<br>Day 4: 50 + 30 + 30 + 30</p><p>Day 10 = 50 + (9 × 30) = 50 + 270 = <b>320 baht</b></p>","fig":null,"page":230}],"questions":[{"label":"Let's Think Along (a)","page":225,"q":"Mike makes the following pattern using square tiles. Draw Figure 5. How many tiles are there in Figure 5?","fig":{"w":382,"h":144.0,"pts":{},"items":[{"t":"poly","p":[[40.0,64.0],[52.0,64.0],[52.0,76.0],[40.0,76.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"txt","at":[46.0,134.0],"s":"Figure 1","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"},{"t":"poly","p":[[118.0,64.0],[130.0,64.0],[130.0,76.0],[118.0,76.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[130.0,52.0],[142.0,52.0],[142.0,64.0],[130.0,64.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[130.0,64.0],[142.0,64.0],[142.0,76.0],[130.0,76.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[130.0,76.0],[142.0,76.0],[142.0,88.0],[130.0,88.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[142.0,64.0],[154.0,64.0],[154.0,76.0],[142.0,76.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"txt","at":[136.0,134.0],"s":"Figure 2","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"},{"t":"poly","p":[[196.0,64.0],[208.0,64.0],[208.0,76.0],[196.0,76.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[208.0,64.0],[220.0,64.0],[220.0,76.0],[208.0,76.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[220.0,40.0],[232.0,40.0],[232.0,52.0],[220.0,52.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[220.0,52.0],[232.0,52.0],[232.0,64.0],[220.0,64.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[220.0,64.0],[232.0,64.0],[232.0,76.0],[220.0,76.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[220.0,76.0],[232.0,76.0],[232.0,88.0],[220.0,88.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[220.0,88.0],[232.0,88.0],[232.0,100.0],[220.0,100.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[232.0,64.0],[244.0,64.0],[244.0,76.0],[232.0,76.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[244.0,64.0],[256.0,64.0],[256.0,76.0],[244.0,76.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"txt","at":[226.0,134.0],"s":"Figure 3","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"},{"t":"poly","p":[[284.0,64.0],[296.0,64.0],[296.0,76.0],[284.0,76.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[296.0,64.0],[308.0,64.0],[308.0,76.0],[296.0,76.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[308.0,64.0],[320.0,64.0],[320.0,76.0],[308.0,76.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[320.0,28.0],[332.0,28.0],[332.0,40.0],[320.0,40.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[320.0,40.0],[332.0,40.0],[332.0,52.0],[320.0,52.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[320.0,52.0],[332.0,52.0],[332.0,64.0],[320.0,64.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[320.0,64.0],[332.0,64.0],[332.0,76.0],[320.0,76.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[320.0,76.0],[332.0,76.0],[332.0,88.0],[320.0,88.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[320.0,88.0],[332.0,88.0],[332.0,100.0],[320.0,100.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[320.0,100.0],[332.0,100.0],[332.0,112.0],[320.0,112.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[332.0,64.0],[344.0,64.0],[344.0,76.0],[332.0,76.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[344.0,64.0],[356.0,64.0],[356.0,76.0],[344.0,76.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"poly","p":[[356.0,64.0],[368.0,64.0],[368.0,76.0],[356.0,76.0]],"fill":"#FFCC80"},{"t":"txt","at":[326.0,134.0],"s":"Figure 4","size":13,"bold":true,"color":"#37474f"}]},"hint":"Count the tiles in each Figure. How many are added each time?","steps":["Figure 1 = <b>1</b> tile, Figure 2 = <b>5</b>, Figure 3 = <b>9</b>, Figure 4 = <b>13</b>.","Each time, 1 tile is added to each of the 4 arms: <b>4 tiles</b> are added.","Figure 5 = 13 + 4 = <b>17</b>. Each arm now has 4 tiles."],"answer":"17 tiles","why":"Figure 5 is a cross with a centre tile and 4 arms of 4 tiles: 1 + 16 = 